CCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID T Wild Type    NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    34G Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    35G Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    37G Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    38G Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    181C Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    182A Wild Type  NRAS  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    183A Wild Type     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    263G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID C Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID A Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID C Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID A Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID G Wild Type    PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    388C Wild Type  PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    517C Wild Type  PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    697C Wild Type  PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID fs   800delA Wild Type     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    524G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    733G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    742C Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    743G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    817C Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    818G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    916C Wild Type    INTERPRETATION     NEGATIVE  No mutations were identified in this sample  including within the EGFR  and KRAS genes     Approximately 50  of lung adenocarcinomas will possess a mutation within at  least one of the tested loci  Ladanyi  REMOVED_CASE_ID    This test does not rule out  mutations in one of the assayed genes which might occur at a low prevalence     TEST INFORMATION   In recent years  major efforts to define the molecular causes of cancer have  revealed a wide number of genetic aberrations  A small subset of these defects   usually referred to as  drivers   is frequently present across cancer types and  appears to be essential for oncogenesis and tumor progression  New drugs  developed to selectively target such cancer promoting pathways  are starting to  complement the more conventional therapeutic approaches  While the clinical  benefit observed with some targeted agents is encouraging  it has become clear  that for such strategies to be successful  it will be necessary to identify the  patient population carrying the genetic abnormalities targeted by each drug     We have developed a multiplexed allele specific assay to detect somatic  mutations in tumor DNA extracted from formalin fixed paraffin embedded samples   Dias Santagata  REMOVED_CASE_ID    The assay SNaPshot Version 3 detects mutations in 68  different loci from 14 cancer genes  Briefly  multiplex PCR using tumor DNA is  followed by mutation detection using single base extension sequencing technology   SNaPshot  Applied Biosystems   In addition  a separate PCR reaction using  primers flanking EGFR exon 19  EGFR exon 20 and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  exon 20 are used to detect  in frame activating insertions or deletions in EGFR and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  that may not be  detected using SNaPshot  The mutations are visualized using an ABI  REMOVED_CASE_ID  DNA  analyzer  The average analytic sensitivity of detecting mutations in this assay  has been established in our laboratory at approximately 5  mutant allele     REFERENCES   Dias Santagata D  Akhavanfard S  David SS  Vernovsky K  Kuhlmann G  Boisvert SL   Stubbs H  McDermott U  Settleman J  Kwak EL  Clark JW  Isakoff SJ  Sequist LV   Engelman JA  Lynch TJ  Haber DA  Louis DN  Ellisen LW  Borger DR  Iafrate AJ    REMOVED_CASE_ID   Rapid targeted mutational analysis of human tumours  a clinical 